The '''History of Zong (1606–1711)''' covers the period from the [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong|Baiduist Expulsion]] to the outbreak of the [[First War of the Isthmus]]. The period was defined by the aftermath of the expulsion — the [[Baidu People's Rebellion]], the mass migration of Baiduists to [[Sinchew]], and the transformation of the colonial society — and by the gradual transition of Zong from an autocratic monarchy to a constitutional one. The [[Merchants' Revolution]] of 1643, a bloodless transition of power within the [[House of Yuanlong]], produced the [[General Assembly]] and the formal participation of non-royal representatives in the legislature, completing the constitutional evolution that had begun with the concessions forced on Emperor Tianren after the Yuanlong restoration.

The period also saw the [[Sinchew War]], in which the Zong mainland fought the Baiduist colony of Sinchew; the [[Lau Kingdom War]], a punitive campaign by Zong against the breakaway Shansi state that had supported the Canglan Rebellion; the [[Kawaei invasion of Zong|Kawaei invasion]], in which Sanese forces under the [[Kawaei Clan]] invaded through Shansi; and the [[Second Lurandian Native Wars]], in which the indigenous peoples of Lurandia, now armed with firearms, renewed their resistance against the colonial powers. The period ended with the outbreak of the [[First War of the Isthmus]] in 1711, which drew Zong into a broader conflict between the Illypnian and Fosian colonial powers.

== The Baidu People's Rebellion (1606–1623) ==

{{Main|Baidu People's Rebellion}}

The [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong|expulsion decree]] of 1606, issued by [[Tianxiao of Zong|Emperor Tianxiao]], banned the practice of [[Baiduism]] within the Kingdom of Zong, ordered the expulsion of all foreign Baiduists, and mandated the forced relocation of Zong Baiduists to the [[Sinchew]] colony. The decree triggered the [[Baidu People's Rebellion]] (1606–1623), a series of Baiduist uprisings that resulted in over 100,000 deaths and has been characterised by modern historians as a genocide.

The rebellion was not a unified movement but a series of localised uprisings that erupted across the kingdom as the expulsion was enforced. The Baiduist communities of the eastern coast, which had been the primary target of the decree, resisted the soldiers dispatched to collect them. The Shansi Baiduist refugees in the southern provinces, who had arrived during the Daaiho War and possessed the most recent experience of armed conflict, formed the most organised and militant resistance. The Baiduist monasteries, which had served as community centres and organisational hubs, functioned as the nodes of the resistance network.

The rebellion was fought with particular ferocity in the southern provinces, where the Shansi refugees had settled. The refugees, who had already lost their homes in the Daaiho War, resisted the expulsion with a desperation that the imperial forces had not anticipated. The matchlock muskets introduced during the Great Peasant Revolt were now in the hands of both the Baiduist militias and the imperial army, and the fighting produced heavy casualties on both sides. The southern provinces, which had only recently begun to recover from the surplus crisis and the Great Peasant Revolt, were devastated once again.

The rebellion was suppressed by 1623, but the suppression did not end the expulsion. The forced relocation of Zong Baiduists to Sinchew continued through the 1620s and 1630s, and by 1650, over one million Zong Baiduists had been transported to the colony. The Sinchew colony, which had been a frontier plantation society, was transformed by the mass influx of Baiduist settlers into a major population centre with its own cultural and political identity — an identity that would, within decades, come into conflict with the mainland that had sent them there.

== The Lau Kingdom War (c. 1612–1615) ==

{{Main|Lau Kingdom War}}

The [[Lau Kingdom]], which had broken away from the collapsing [[The Jyunhap|Jyunhap]] federation in 1605 and had served as the base for the failed [[Canglan Rebellion]], was the target of a punitive campaign by the Kingdom of Zong in the early 1610s. Emperor Tianxiao, who had suppressed the Canglan Rebellion and issued the expulsion decree, regarded the Lau Kingdom's continued existence as an affront to the dynasty and a threat to the kingdom's southern frontier. The Lau Kingdom had provided the base, the arms, and the Shansi fighters for the rebellion that had assassinated Emperor Tianren — Tianxiao's elder brother — and the court considered the Lau King's survival unfinished business.

The Zong campaign was not a full-scale conquest but an operation of destabilisation. Tianxiao's objective was not to annex the Lau Kingdom's territory — a Shansi state on the far side of the frontier, inhabited by a Sansei-speaking population that Zong had no desire to govern — but to weaken the Lau Kingdom sufficiently to prevent it from serving as a base for future Baiduist operations against Zong. Zong forces crossed the southern frontier and conducted a series of punitive raids into Lau territory, targeting the Lau King's military installations, supply depots, and the Baiduist networks that had supported the Canglan Rebellion.

The campaign was constrained by the ongoing Baidu People's Rebellion on the mainland. The Zong military, already stretched thin by the suppression of the Baiduist uprisings and the enforcement of the expulsion, could not commit sufficient forces to a sustained operation across the frontier. The Lau Kingdom, familiar with the Shansi terrain and supported by the local Baiduist population, resisted the Zong incursions through guerrilla tactics and cross-border skirmishing. The conflict settled into a pattern of punitive raids and counter-raids that lasted until approximately 1615, when both sides — exhausted by the fighting and facing other pressures — agreed to a ceasefire. The ceasefire did not resolve the underlying tensions, and the Lau Kingdom remained a source of instability on the southern frontier for decades.

== The Kawaei invasion (1620–1624) ==

{{Main|Kawaei invasion of Zong}}

In 1620, [[Kawaei Yoshiyuki]], having unified the southern court of the Sanese archipelago, launched a military campaign against the Kingdom of Zong. The invasion was conceived as a trans-oceanic operation using [[Shansi]] as a staging ground for an advance into Zong territory. Despite opposition from his vassals, who questioned the logistical feasibility of projecting force across the strait against a continental power, Yoshiyuki assembled an expeditionary force of approximately 80,000 soldiers drawn from various clans and dispatched it from the western tip of [[Suzuya]] island.

The invasion force landed on the southeastern coast of Shansi, in the territory of the [[Wong Kingdom]] — the largest of the former Jyunhap states, formed by the merger of the Wong and Leung States that had triggered the Daaiho War. The Wong Kingdom, although the dominant power in the post-Jyunhap Shansi, had been exhausted by over a decade of continental warfare and was unable to mount an effective defence against the landing. The Sanese forces, battle-hardened by the [[Sengoku Period]] and equipped with matchlock muskets, rapidly overran the Wong Kingdom's coastal settlements and advanced inland along the river valleys.

The invasion threatened the southern frontier of Zong. Emperor Tianxiao, who had only recently suppressed the Baidu People's Rebellion and concluded the Lau Kingdom War, reinforced the southern garrisons and dispatched a relief army to the frontier. The Zong military, however, was stretched thin by the ongoing enforcement of the Baiduist expulsion and could not commit sufficient forces to a full-scale interdiction of the Sanese advance.

The Kawaei campaign did not reach Zong territory. The Sanese forces became bogged down in the Shansi interior, where the fragmented political landscape and the devastated terrain impeded sustained campaigning. The Shansi states, despite their disunity, resisted the invasion using the guerrilla tactics that had characterised the Daaiho War. The [[Lau Kingdom]], whose own territory was threatened by the Sanese advance, redirected its forces against the invaders, creating a secondary front that further stretched the Kawaei expedition. By 1624, the Sanese forces had been contained in a coastal enclave in southeastern Shansi, and Yoshiyuki's campaign against Zong had been effectively neutralised.

The invasion, while it did not penetrate Zong territory, had significant strategic consequences. It demonstrated that the Sanese archipelago — which had been a source of mercenaries and commercial exchange for Zong — could also project military power against the kingdom. The campaign further destabilised the Shansi region, producing a renewed wave of refugees into southern Zong, and compelled the Zong court to maintain an enhanced military presence on the southern frontier at a time when the kingdom's resources were already strained by the expulsion and its aftermath.

== Tianxiao's later reign (1624–1643) ==

The decades following the suppression of the Baidu People's Rebellion and the Kawaei invasion were a period of consolidation and exhaustion. Emperor Tianxiao, who had issued the expulsion decree and overseen the suppression of the rebellion, continued to rule with the Tianist hardliners dominant in the court. The expulsion continued through the 1620s and 1630s, and the Baiduist population of the mainland was steadily reduced as more communities were rounded up and transported to Sinchew.

The later years of Tianxiao's reign were marked by growing tension between the throne and the merchant elite. The expulsion had removed a significant portion of the merchant class — the Baiduist merchants of the eastern coast had been among the most commercially active in the kingdom — and the economic consequences of their removal became increasingly apparent. The Shendan trade networks, which had been operated largely by Baiduist merchant houses, were disrupted. The colonial trade, which depended on the expertise and capital of the eastern merchant elite, suffered. The court's attempts to replace the expelled Baiduist merchants with Tianist loyalists produced a less experienced and less connected commercial class, and the kingdom's trade revenues declined.

The Societal Assembly, which had been expanded under Tianren's concessions and now included colonial representatives from Louyang and Sinchew, increasingly chafed under Tianxiao's autocratic rule. The assembly's legislative authority, which had been formally expanded after the restoration, was in practice circumvented by the emperor's reliance on decree and edict. The colonial representatives, who had been granted seats in recognition of their support for the restoration, found that their voices were ignored on matters of colonial policy. The merchant representatives, who had been seated under the integration policy and had survived the expulsion by renouncing Baiduism, found that their commercial interests were subordinated to the Tianist establishment's agenda.

Tianxiao died in approximately 1643, after a reign of nearly four decades. His death, which might have been expected to produce a smooth succession within the [[House of Yuanlong]], instead triggered the [[Merchants' Revolution]].

== The Merchants' Revolution and the constitutional monarchy (1643) ==

{{Main|Merchants' Revolution}}

Tianxiao's designated heir was his eldest son, a Tianist hardliner who had been groomed to continue his father's policies. The merchant elite of the eastern coast, the colonial representatives, and the non-Tianist members of the Societal Assembly viewed the prospect of another Tianist reign with alarm. The economic decline that had followed the expulsion, the marginalisation of the assembly, and the growing evidence that the Tianist establishment's policies were harming the kingdom's commercial and colonial interests had produced a consensus among the commercial elite that the succession could not be permitted to follow the established pattern.

The [[Merchants' Revolution]] was a bloodless transition of power engineered by a coalition of merchant houses, colonial representatives, and sympathetic aristocrats within the [[House of Yuanlong]]. The coalition identified a third cousin of the late emperor — a Yuanlong family member who had been marginalised by the Tianist court and who had aligned himself with the merchant interests of the eastern coast. The third cousin, who had served as a commercial administrator in the [[Louyang]] colony and had developed close ties to the colonial merchant elite, was positioned as an alternative candidate for the throne.

The transition was accomplished through the Societal Assembly. The assembly, exercising the legislative authority that had been formally expanded under Tianren's concessions but had remained dormant under Tianxiao, convened without the emperor's summons — a right that had been granted but never exercised — and passed a resolution recognising the third cousin as the legitimate heir, bypassing Tianxiao's designated successor. The designated heir, lacking the military or political support to challenge the assembly's decision, was persuaded to renounce his claim. The third cousin was proclaimed as [[Tianrong of Zong|Emperor Tianrong]] (天榮, "Heavenly Prosperity").

The Merchants' Revolution was not a revolution in the radical sense — it did not overthrow the dynasty or abolish the monarchy. It was a palace transition, accomplished through institutional mechanisms rather than military force, that shifted the balance of power within the existing constitutional framework. Its consequences, however, were profound. The new emperor, who owed his throne to the merchant coalition and the assembly, was required to accept a further expansion of the assembly's authority that completed the transition to a constitutional monarchy.

=== The General Assembly and the constitutional framework ===

The most significant innovation of the Merchants' Revolution was the creation of the [[General Assembly]] (大會), a reformed legislature that formalised the participation of non-royal representatives in the governance of the kingdom. The Societal Assembly, which had previously been composed of royal appointees and aristocratic representatives, was reconstituted as the General Assembly, with expanded membership that included elected representatives of the localities, the merchant guilds, and the colonial administrations.

The precedent for non-royal representation had been established by the concessions to Louyang and Sinchew after the Yuanlong restoration, when the colonial administrations — whose representatives were royal appointees rather than royals themselves — had been granted seats in the Societal Assembly. The Merchants' Revolution formalised and extended this precedent to the mainland. Localities across the kingdom were granted the right to elect representatives to the General Assembly, chosen from the local gentry, merchant class, and professional elite. The representatives were not required to be members of the Yuanlong dynasty or the Tianist aristocracy, and the assembly's deliberations were opened to non-royal voices for the first time in the kingdom's history.

The constitutional framework that emerged from the Merchants' Revolution was not a full parliamentary system. The emperor retained significant powers: the right to appoint and dismiss ministers, to command the military, to conduct foreign policy, and to issue edicts on matters of public order and defence. The Tianist church remained the state religion, and the emperor's legitimacy continued to rest on the Mandate of Heaven. The General Assembly's authority, however, was now codified rather than customary: the assembly's ratification was required for all legislation, taxation, and succession decisions, and the assembly gained the right to initiate legislation, to audit the court's expenditure, and to summon ministers for questioning. The emperor could no longer govern by decree alone, and the General Assembly became a co-equal branch of the government rather than an advisory body.

The transition to constitutional monarchy was not universally welcomed. The Tianist hardliners, who had dominated the court under Tianxiao, viewed the expansion of the assembly's authority as a diminution of the emperor's divine mandate and a surrender to commercial interests. The western and southern aristocracy, which had been the traditional power base of the Tianist establishment, was particularly alarmed by the empowerment of the merchant class and the localities. The tensions between the constitutional monarchy and the Tianist establishment would persist throughout the period and shape the politics of the late 17th century.

=== Architects of the General Assembly ===

The creation of the General Assembly was not the product of a single individual but the work of a coalition of merchants, scholars, colonial administrators, and sympathetic aristocrats who provided the political vision, the institutional expertise, and the practical organisation necessary to transform the Societal Assembly into a constitutional legislature. Several figures played critical roles in the transition.

[[Lim Chiau-beng]] (林朝明, 1598–1672) was the merchant house leader who organised the coalition that engineered the Merchants' Revolution. The head of the [[Lim merchant house]], one of the surviving eastern coast trading families that had renounced Baiduism to avoid the expulsion, Lim had built a commercial network that connected the eastern ports to the Louyang colony and the Shendan trade. Though he had conformed to the Tianist establishment to preserve his business, Lim had maintained covert ties to the Baiduist merchant diaspora in Sinchew and to the colonial merchant elite in Louyang. His position — a Tianist-conforming merchant with Baiduist sympathies and colonial connections — made him the natural broker between the factions that coalesced against Tianxiao's designated heir. Lim organised the secret meetings between the merchant houses, the colonial representatives, and the Yuanlong aristocrats that produced the plan for the succession, and he provided the financial resources that secured the loyalty of the eastern port garrisons during the transition. After the Merchants' Revolution, Lim served as the first elected representative of the eastern port cities in the General Assembly and was instrumental in drafting the procedural rules that governed the assembly's deliberations.

[[Ng Hong-sik]] (黃弘錫, 1605–1668) was the constitutional theorist who provided the intellectual framework for the General Assembly. A scholar of the eastern coast who had studied both Tianist political theology and the Baiduist canonical traditions, Ng had spent the 1630s and 1640s writing a series of treatises — collectively known as the ''[[Assembly Discourses]]'' (議會論) — that argued for the formal participation of non-royal representatives in the legislature. Ng's argument was rooted in both Tianist and Baiduist intellectual traditions: from the Tianist side, he drew on the concept of the Mandate of Heaven to argue that the mandate required the emperor to govern in consultation with the assembled representatives of the people; from the Baiduist side, he drew on the monastic tradition of communal decision-making to argue that the assembly should include representatives chosen by the communities they served rather than appointed by the crown. The ''Assembly Discourses'' circulated in manuscript form among the merchant elite and the colonial administrators before the Merchants' Revolution and provided the constitutional theory that the coalition invoked when it reconstituted the Societal Assembly as the General Assembly. After the transition, Ng was appointed as the first [[Chancellor of the Assembly]], a position responsible for moderating the assembly's debates and certifying its resolutions.

[[Tan Eng-liong]] (陳永龍, 1590–1661) was the colonial representative who bridged the mainland and the colonial interests. A Louyang-born merchant who had served as the colonial administration's commercial liaison to the mainland court, Tan had been one of the colonial representatives seated in the Societal Assembly under the concessions granted after the Yuanlong restoration. His experience in the assembly — where the colonial representatives had been consistently marginalised by the Tianist establishment — had convinced him that the constitutional framework needed to be reformed to give the colonies a genuine voice in the governance of the kingdom. Tan was the architect of the colonial representation system that was incorporated into the General Assembly: he designed the electoral districts for Louyang and Sinchew, established the procedures by which the colonial representatives were chosen, and negotiated the terms under which the colonial administrations accepted the new constitutional framework. Tan's contribution was particularly significant because he ensured that the General Assembly's colonial representatives were elected by the colonial merchant elite and the colonial administrations rather than appointed by the crown, giving the colonies a degree of independence from the mainland that the earlier royal-appointee system had not provided.

[[Ong Tek-khiam]] (王德謙, 1585–1659) was the Yuanlong aristocrat who provided the dynastic legitimacy for the transition. A distant cousin of Emperor Tianxiao and a member of the cadet branch of the [[House of Yuanlong]] that had been marginalised by the Tianist court, Ong had served in the provincial administration of the northern provinces and had developed a reputation for competent governance and commercial sympathy. Ong's role in the coalition was to identify and cultivate the third cousin who would become Emperor Tianrong — a task that required him to navigate the complex family politics of the Yuanlong dynasty and to ensure that the alternative candidate had sufficient dynastic legitimacy to be accepted by the aristocratic establishment. Ong also played a critical role in persuading the designated heir to renounce his claim, using his family connections and his reputation within the dynasty to broker a bloodless resolution. After the transition, Ong was appointed as the first [[President of the General Assembly]] (大會主席), a position that combined the ceremonial role of presiding over the assembly's sessions with the substantive authority of representing the assembly in its dealings with the emperor.

== The Restoration Attempt (1653) ==

The constitutional monarchy established by the Merchants' Revolution was not universally accepted, and the Tianist hardliners who had dominated the court under Tianxiao regarded the new order as an illegitimate usurpation of the emperor's divine mandate. The designated heir — Tianxiao's eldest son, who had been persuaded to renounce his claim after the Merchants' Revolution — had withdrawn to the western provinces, where the Tianist aristocracy had been the traditional power base of the absolutist establishment. Though he had formally renounced his claim, he remained a focal point for the disaffected elements of the old order: the western and southern aristocracy, the Tianist clergy, and the military garrisons that had been loyal to Tianxiao's regime.

The Sinchew War, which erupted in 1648 and consumed the colonial military resources of the kingdom, provided the opportunity the absolutist faction had been waiting for. The mainland reinforcements dispatched to Sinchew had weakened the garrisons of the capital and the northern provinces, and the protracted nature of the colonial conflict had produced growing discontent with the constitutional government's handling of the war. The General Assembly's commercial representatives, who had been the driving force behind the Merchants' Revolution, were increasingly criticised for prioritising colonial commercial interests over the security of the mainland. The Tianist hardliners argued that the constitutional monarchy had weakened the kingdom's ability to respond to the colonial crisis and that only the restoration of absolute imperial authority could produce the decisive action that the situation demanded.

In 1653, with the Sinchew War at its height and the mainland forces bogged down in the colonial conflict, the designated heir declared himself emperor, repudiating his renunciation and claiming that it had been extracted under duress. The declaration was issued from the western provincial capital, where the Tianist aristocracy and the local garrisons had rallied to his cause. The self-proclaimed emperor — who took the era name [[Tianwei of Zong|Tianwei]] (天威, "Heavenly Authority") — was supported by a coalition of western and southern aristocrats, Tianist clergy, and garrison commanders who had been marginalised by the constitutional transition. The Restoration Attempt, as it became known, was an attempt to overthrow the constitutional monarchy and restore the autocratic rule that had characterised Tianxiao's reign.

The attempt, however, failed. Emperor Tianrong, though distracted by the Sinchew War, retained the support of the merchant militia of the eastern coast, the General Assembly, and the majority of the northern and eastern garrisons. [[Ong Tek-khiam]], the President of the General Assembly, rallied the assembly to declare Tianwei a traitor and authorise the deployment of loyalist forces. [[Lim Chiau-beng]], the merchant house leader who had organised the Merchants' Revolution, used his commercial networks to finance the loyalist response and to secure the loyalty of the eastern port garrisons. The loyalist forces, drawn from the eastern merchant militia and the northern garrisons, marched against the western rebels and defeated them in a brief but decisive campaign in the autumn of 1653. The western garrisons, though loyal to the Tianist establishment, were outnumbered and outmanoeuvred, and the aristocratic coalition collapsed within weeks of the first engagement.

Tianwei was captured, tried by the General Assembly, and stripped of his dynastic titles. He was exiled to a remote monastery in the northern provinces, where he lived under supervision until his death in 1671. The failure of the Restoration Attempt consolidated the constitutional monarchy and demonstrated that the merchant coalition and the General Assembly possessed the political and military resources to defend the new order. The western and southern aristocracy, though not reconciled to the constitutional monarchy, was sufficiently intimidated to refrain from further armed challenges during Tianrong's reign. The Tianist clergy, which had broadly supported the restoration attempt, was placed under increased supervision by the constitutional government, and the Tianist church's political influence was curtailed.

The Restoration Attempt also had consequences for the Sinchew War. The diversion of loyalist forces to suppress the western rebellion had further strained the mainland's military resources and prolonged the colonial conflict. The Baiduist rebels in Sinchew, who had been aware of the restoration attempt, had launched a coordinated offensive during the crisis, exploiting the mainland's distraction to seize additional territory. The failure of the restoration attempt, however, meant that the constitutional government emerged from the crisis with its authority enhanced, and the subsequent conduct of the Sinchew War was shaped by a government that was more, rather than less, committed to the constitutional order.

== The Sinchew War (1648–1656) ==

{{Main|Sinchew War}}

The mass relocation of over one million Zong Baiduists to the [[Sinchew]] colony, completed by 1650, transformed the colonial society. The Baiduist settlers, expelled from the mainland with little more than what they could carry, arrived in Sinchew to find a frontier plantation society ill-equipped to absorb them. The early years of the relocation were marked by disease, starvation, and conflict with the existing colonial population and the indigenous peoples of the northwestern coast.

The Baiduist settlers, however, were not a passive population. They included merchants, artisans, soldiers, and monks — the same eastern coast elite that had operated the Shendan trade networks and the Baiduist monastic institutions. Within a decade of the expulsion, the Baiduist settlers had established their own economic and political institutions in Sinchew: monasteries that functioned as banks and warehouses, merchant houses that reconstituted the Shendan trade networks from the colonial side, and militias organised along the lines of the Baiduist resistance forces that had fought the imperial army during the Great Peasant Revolt and the Baidu People's Rebellion.

The emergence of the Baiduist settler institutions produced a dual power structure within the Sinchew colony. The older colonial establishment — the colonial administration, the plantation owners, the Sanese mercenary garrisons, and the merchant houses that had operated the plantation economy before the expulsion — retained the formal authority of government but found itself increasingly marginalised by the Baiduist institutions that had emerged among the settler population. The Baiduist merchant houses, which had reconstituted the Shendan trade networks, were generating wealth that flowed through Baiduist monasteries rather than through the colonial administration. The Baiduist militias, better organised and better armed than the colonial garrisons, were effectively the dominant military force in the colony. The older colonial establishment, whose plantation economy depended on the enslaved labour force brought through the Secret Trade and whose authority derived from the mainland court, viewed the Baiduist settlers as a threat to both their economic interests and their political control.

The tension between the two communities — the older colonial establishment and the new Baiduist settlers — was the internal dimension of the Sinchew War. The colonial administration, under pressure from the mainland court, attempted to assert its authority over the Baiduist institutions — demanding that the monasteries submit to colonial inspection, that the militias disarm, and that the Baiduist merchant houses pay taxes to the colonial administration rather than to the monasteries. The Baiduist settlers, who had been expelled from the mainland by the same government that was now demanding their submission, refused. The older colonial establishment, which had its own grievances against the Baiduist settlers — particularly the competition for land, resources, and commercial control — supported the colonial administration's demands and provided the local intelligence and logistical infrastructure for the confrontation.

The [[Sinchew War]] thus had two interconnected dimensions: an internal civil war within the Sinchew colony between the older colonial establishment and the new Baiduist settler community, and an external war between the Zong mainland — supported by the [[Louyang]] colony and the Sinchew colonial loyalists — and the Sinchew Baiduist rebels.

The war began in 1648, when the colonial administration, reinforced by mainland troops dispatched by Emperor Tianrong, moved to disarm the Baiduist militias. The Baiduist militias resisted, and the colony descended into armed conflict. On the loyalist side, the combatants comprised the Sinchew colonial administration and its garrisons, the mainland reinforcements dispatched across the Shendan Ocean, the Sinchew colonial loyalists drawn from the older colonial establishment — the plantation owners, the pre-expulsion merchant houses, and the Sanese mercenary garrisons — and the [[Louyang]] colonial administration, which sided with the mainland. On the rebel side, the combatants comprised the Baiduist settler militias, organised through the monastic networks and armed with the matchlock muskets that had become standard infantry weapons, and the Baiduist merchant houses, which provided the financial and logistical infrastructure for the resistance.

The [[Louyang]] colony, which had supported the Yuanlong restoration and had been granted representation in the General Assembly, sided with the mainland against the Sinchew rebels. Louyang's plantation economy, which was integrated with the mainland trade, had little sympathy for the Baiduist settlers who had been expelled from the mainland. The Louyang colonial administration provided naval support, supplies, and intelligence to the mainland forces, and the Louyang garrisons were deployed to reinforce the Sinchew colonial administration. The Louyang forces also played a critical role in blockading the Sinchew coast, restricting the Baiduist rebels' access to external supply lines and preventing the coordination of the Baiduist settler networks with any potential continental allies.

The internal civil war within Sinchew was fought with particular bitterness. The older colonial establishment and the Baiduist settlers had been competing for control of the colony's economic resources — land, labour, and trade routes — for over a decade, and the war provided the occasion for a settling of accounts. Plantation owners who had employed enslaved labour brought through the Secret Trade found their estates occupied by Baiduist settler militias. The Sanese mercenaries of the Sea Wolves, who had been the backbone of the colonial garrison system, were deployed against the Baiduist settlers but found themselves fighting a population that was better armed and better organised than the indigenous forces they had been accustomed to combating. The colonial loyalists — the pre-expulsion merchant houses and the plantation elite — formed irregular units that fought alongside the colonial garrisons, but their numbers were insufficient to match the Baiduist settler militia.

The Sinchew War was a protracted conflict that lasted for eight years. The Baiduist militias, fighting on their home territory and motivated by the memory of the expulsion, proved difficult to defeat. The mainland forces, fighting at the end of a long supply line across the Shendan Ocean, were unable to project sufficient force to overwhelm the Baiduist resistance. The internal civil war further complicated the mainland's position: the colonial loyalists, though aligned with the mainland, were primarily motivated by their own economic interests rather than by loyalty to the Zong crown, and their cooperation with the mainland forces was inconsistent. The war settled into a pattern of sieges, raids, and negotiated truces that gradually eroded the Baiduist position but did not produce a decisive victory for either side.

The war ended in 1656 with a negotiated settlement. The Baiduist settlers were granted a degree of self-governance within the Sinchew colony — the right to maintain their monastic institutions, to organise their own militias for local defence, and to manage their own commercial networks — in exchange for recognising the sovereignty of the Zong crown and paying an annual tribute to the colonial administration. The settlement was a compromise that neither side found fully satisfactory: the mainland had failed to fully subjugate the Baiduist settlers, and the Baiduists had been forced to acknowledge the authority of the government that had expelled them. The older colonial establishment, which had expected the mainland to restore its pre-eminence in the colony, found that the settlement left the Baiduist institutions intact and the dual power structure unresolved. The tensions produced by the war would persist, and the self-governance arrangements would become the foundation for the increasing autonomy of Sinchew in the following decades.

== The Second Lurandian Native Wars (1650s–1670s) ==

{{Main|Second Lurandian Native Wars}}

The [[Second Lurandian Native Wars]] were a series of conflicts between the indigenous peoples of [[Lurandia]] and the Fosian colonial powers — primarily Zong, but also the [[Kingdom of Daras|Darasian]] colonial settlements in southern Lurandia — that erupted in the mid-17th century. The wars were a continuation of the [[First Lurandian Native Wars]] of 1538–1560s, but with a critical difference: by the 1650s, the indigenous peoples of Lurandia had acquired firearms through the smuggling networks established during the first wars and expanded through the Volisanian colony of [[Balboa]] and the Darasian colonial presence in the south.

The First Lurandian Native Wars had seen the Volisanian authorities in Balboa smuggling firearms to the indigenous Lurandians as part of a strategy to weaken the Zong colonies. The smuggling networks had continued through the intervening decades, and by the mid-17th century, the indigenous peoples of the Lurandian interior had developed a substantial firearms capability. The matchlock muskets introduced through the smuggling networks had been supplemented by flintlock mechanisms, which were more reliable and faster to reload, and the indigenous warriors had developed tactics that exploited the terrain of the Lurandian forests and river valleys to maximum effect.

The Second Lurandian Native Wars were not a separate conflict but emerged directly out of the instability produced by the [[Sinchew War]]. The Sinchew War, which had consumed the colonial garrisons and the Baiduist militias in a protracted internal and external conflict from 1648 to 1656, had drawn the colonial military resources away from the interior frontier. The garrisons that had been stationed along the boundary between the colonial settlements and the indigenous territories were redeployed to the Sinchew War, leaving the frontier undefended. The indigenous peoples, who had been watching the colonial expansion encroach on their hunting grounds and settlements for over a decade, seized the opportunity created by the colonial military's preoccupation with the Sinchew War to reclaim territory that had been lost to the colonial settlements during the preceding decades.

The Sinchew War thus transitioned into the Second Lurandian Native Wars. As the fighting between the colonial administration, the mainland forces, and the Baiduist settlers consumed the colony's military resources, the indigenous peoples of the Lurandian interior launched coordinated raids on the colonial settlements and supply lines. The Louyang colony, which had committed its garrisons to the Sinchew War in support of the mainland, found its own frontier exposed to indigenous raids. The colonial administrations, already stretched thin by the Sinchew War and the ongoing tensions between the older colonial establishment and the Baiduist settlers, were unable to contain the indigenous resistance. The Sanese mercenaries of the Sea Wolves, who had been the backbone of the colonial garrison system, were increasingly deployed against the indigenous forces, but the mercenaries' conventional tactics were ill-suited to the forest warfare of the Lurandian interior. The colonial forces, equipped with flintlock muskets and artillery, possessed a technological advantage in open engagement, but the indigenous warriors' knowledge of the terrain and their ability to melt into the forests after conducting raids negated much of this advantage.

The fighting was brutal, and the indigenous peoples suffered heavy casualties — not only from combat but from the disease, displacement, and starvation that accompanied the disruption of their hunting grounds and settlements. Thousands of indigenous Lurandians were killed during the wars, and the population of the interior declined sharply as communities were destroyed or forced to retreat deeper into the forests. The colonial settlements also suffered significant losses, as the indigenous raids destroyed outlying plantations and supply lines and forced the abandonment of settlements that had been established in the interior during the preceding decades.

The Second Lurandian Native Wars gradually subsided in the 1670s, as the colonial administrations, having concluded the Sinchew War and consolidated their forces, negotiated a series of treaties with the indigenous peoples that established boundaries between the colonial settlements and the indigenous territories. The treaties, which were often broken within years of their signing, did not produce a lasting peace. The underlying conflicts — the competition for land, the encroachment of the colonial settlements on indigenous hunting grounds, and the mutual suspicion produced by decades of warfare — remained unresolved. The treaties reduced the intensity of the conflict and allowed the colonial settlements to consolidate their control over the coastal regions, but the interior remained under indigenous influence, and the border between the colonial and indigenous spheres was a zone of intermittent raiding, skirmishing, and uneasy coexistence rather than a stable frontier. The unresolved conflicts would persist into the 18th century and would not be definitively addressed until the colonial consolidations of the 19th century.

== The end of the Secret Trade (1680) ==

{{Main|The Secret Trade}}

The [[Secret Trade]] — the illicit trade in enslaved peoples that had supplied the Sinchew plantation economy with labour from [[Dagit]] and the [[Balausia Peninsula]] since the 1570s — was suppressed in 1680, when diplomatic pressure from the [[Sultanate of Maros]] compelled the Zong court to deploy naval forces against the slave raiders. The Sultanate of Maros, whose peripheral territories had been targeted by the raiders for decades, had formally protested to Zong in the 1670s, presenting documentation of raids and evidence of enslaved peoples in Sinchew.

The Zong court, now governed under the constitutional monarchy and more responsive to the General Assembly's commercial interests than the Tianist court had been, authorised naval action in 1680. The Zong navy was deployed to patrol the waters between the Balausian Peninsula and Sinchew, with explicit orders to interdict slave raiders. The naval presence disrupted the large-scale raiding operations, though smaller-scale raids continued in secret until the 1750s, by which time the enslaved population of Sinchew had become largely self-reproducing through natural increase.

The end of the Secret Trade marked a shift in the Zong court's posture toward the colonial enterprise. Under the constitutional monarchy, the General Assembly's commercial representatives — who were more concerned with the legitimacy and sustainability of the colonial trade than with the short-term profits of the plantation economy — pushed for the suppression of the trade and the improvement of the colonial administration's governance. The end of the Secret Trade was a consequence of this shift, and it reflected the growing influence of the merchant class in the constitutional government.

Emperor Tianrong died in 1681, after a reign of nearly four decades that had seen the consolidation of the constitutional monarchy, the suppression of the Restoration Attempt, the Sinchew War, and the end of the Secret Trade. He was succeeded by his son, who was proclaimed as [[Tianzheng of Zong|Emperor Tianzheng]] (天正, "Heavenly Correctness"). Tianzheng's reign, which lasted until 1711, was a period of relative stability on the mainland but of growing tension in the colonial sphere. The constitutional framework established by the Merchants' Revolution functioned without major challenge — the failure of the Restoration Attempt had discouraged further armed opposition to the constitutional order, and the General Assembly had become an accepted institution of governance. The unresolved tensions in the colonial sphere, however — the dual power structure in Sinchew, the unresolved indigenous conflicts in Lurandia, and the growing competition with the Illypnian colonial powers over the Isthmus of Balboa — would culminate in the outbreak of the First War of the Isthmus in the final year of Tianzheng's reign.

== The First War of the Isthmus (1711) ==

{{Main|First War of the Isthmus}}

The [[First War of the Isthmus]] erupted in 1711, the culmination of decades of escalating tension between the Fosian and Illypnian colonial powers over the [[Isthmus of Balboa]], the narrow land bridge connecting the continents of [[Lurandia]] and [[Abyala]]. The war's causes were rooted in two interconnected grievances: Volisanian interference in the Zong colonial sphere during the Sinchew War, and the closure of Volisanian ports to Zong commercial traffic.

During the Sinchew War (1648–1656), the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisania]] had covertly supported the Sinchew Baiduist rebels as part of a broader strategy to weaken the Zong colonial position in Lurandia. The Volisanian colonial authorities in [[Balboa]], who controlled the isthmus and the principal ports on the Shendan coast, had facilitated the supply of firearms and materiel to the Baiduist militias through the smuggling networks that had been established during the First Lurandian Native Wars. The Volisanian support was not sufficient to alter the outcome of the war — the Sinchew War ended in a negotiated settlement that left the Zong crown's sovereignty intact — but it prolonged the conflict and increased the casualties on both sides. The Zong court, which discovered the extent of Volisanian involvement in the post-war settlement negotiations, regarded the interference as an act of covert aggression.

The second grievance was the closure of Volisanian ports to Zong commercial traffic. The Volisanian colonial administration, which controlled [[Sant Cristòfor]] — the principal port on the Shendan coast of the isthmus and the key transit point for trade between the Fosian and Illypnian worlds — had increasingly restricted Zong merchant access to the port throughout the late 17th century. The closures were part of a Volisanian strategy to monopolise the trans-isthmian trade and to marginalise the Fosian colonial powers in the Illypnian commercial networks. For the Zong merchant elite, who had been the driving force behind the Merchants' Revolution and the constitutional monarchy, the closure of Sant Cristòfor was a direct threat to the commercial interests that the General Assembly had been created to protect.

The [[An-Ming Understanding]] of 1605–1606 had assigned the isthmus as a neutral zone between the Zong and Marosan spheres, but the expansion of the colonial settlements and the growing strategic importance of the isthmus as a transit route between the Shendan and Aethiopean coasts had made the neutral zone increasingly contested. The Zong government, governed under the constitutional monarchy and pressured by the General Assembly's commercial representatives to secure access to the Illypnian trade networks, determined to seize Sant Cristòfor by force.

In 1711, Emperor Tianzheng authorised an expedition to capture Sant Cristòfor. The attack represented a direct challenge to Illypnian colonial dominance in the region. Volisania, the preeminent Illypnian naval power, responded with a full mobilisation of its forces. [[Burawa]], bound to Volisania by a strategic alignment, committed naval forces, ground troops, and financial resources to support the Illypnian coalition. The war quickly expanded beyond a local colonial skirmish: [[Breisland]] and [[Merica]], two Illypnian powers that had historically competed with Volisania for colonial advantage, chose to support Zong rather than their fellow Illypnians, calculating that a Volisanian defeat would weaken the kingdom that dominated Illypnian colonial politics.

The Zong colonial settlements in Lurandia, the closest Fosian colonies to the isthmus, were the first to be engaged. The colonial garrisons of Sinchew and Louyang, strengthened by the Second Lurandian Native Wars and the Sinchew War, were deployed to defend the Zong territorial claims, and the mainland court dispatched reinforcements across the Shendan Ocean. Zong forces succeeded in blockading Sant Cristòfor in 1713 and eventually captured the port after a sustained siege. The Illypnian coalition, however, mounted a counter-offensive that surrounded the Zong expeditionary force. Cut off from reinforcement and supply, the Zong position became untenable, and the Zong court opened negotiations.

The outbreak of the First War of the Isthmus in 1711 marked the end of the period covered by this article. The war itself, which lasted until 1716, ended with the [[Treaty of Remoria]], a compromise that saw Zong withdraw from Sant Cristòfor but receive docking and passage rights through Volisanian ports — the access to the Illypnian trade networks that Zong had sought all along. For Volisania, the outcome was humiliating: the kingdom had mobilised its vast naval resources, committed to a major war, and emerged having granted its opponent the very concessions that the war had been fought to prevent. The perception that Volisania had been outmanoeuvred at the negotiating table contributed to domestic unrest that would culminate in the Volisanian Civil War of 1736. The war reshaped the colonial landscape of the New World and drew the Fosian powers into a sustained conflict with the Illypnian colonial empires that would define the 18th century.

== Society and culture ==

The period from 1606 to 1711 produced deep cultural transformations in Zong, driven by the expulsion, the mass migration, the constitutional transition, and the ongoing colonial enterprise.

=== The Baiduist diaspora and Sinchew creole culture ===

The mass relocation of over one million Zong Baiduists to Sinchew produced a distinctive creole culture in the colony that blended Zong, Sanese, indigenous, and enslaved traditions. The Baiduist settlers, who brought the monastic culture, the commercial networks, and the literary traditions of the eastern coast, established institutions in Sinchew that replicated and adapted the structures they had lost on the mainland. The Sinchew monasteries, which functioned as religious, commercial, and educational institutions, became the centres of a new Baiduist culture shaped by the frontier experience and the trauma of the expulsion.

The Sinchew creole culture was also shaped by the interaction between the Baiduist settlers and the existing colonial population. The Sanese mercenaries of the Sea Wolves, who had been the backbone of the colonial garrison system, brought their own martial traditions and religious practices — particularly Iron Baiduism, which found common cause with the Baiduist settlers. The enslaved peoples from Dagit and the Balausia Peninsula, brought to Sinchew through the Secret Trade, introduced their own cultural traditions — music, food, oral storytelling, and religious practices — into the colonial society. The cultural exchange between these communities produced a society increasingly distinct from the mainland.

=== The constitutional culture and the General Assembly ===

The transition to constitutional monarchy produced a new political culture in Zong. The General Assembly, which now included non-royal representatives from the localities, the merchant guilds, and the colonial administrations, became a forum for debate and deliberation qualitatively different from the aristocratic council that had preceded it. The assembly's debates, recorded and circulated in printed form for the first time during this period, introduced a new genre of political literature — the assembly speech — that was read and discussed in the port cities and the provincial towns.

The printed circulation of the assembly's debates was a consequence of the expansion of printing technology, which had been introduced to Zong through the continental trade routes and adopted by the merchant houses for commercial purposes. The use of printing for political purposes — the publication of assembly debates, political pamphlets, and commercial treatises — produced a public sphere that was broader and more engaged than the court-centric political culture of the earlier period. The constitutional monarchy, by creating institutions accessible to non-royal participants, also created a culture of political participation that would expand in the following centuries.

=== Literature and the literature of exile ===

The expulsion produced a body of literature defined by the experience of dispossession and exile. The Baiduist poets of Sinchew, writing in the decades after the expulsion, produced a literature of longing and loss that recalled the eastern coast monasteries, the Shendan trade, and the integrated life that the Baiduist community had known on the mainland. The "Exile Songs" (流亡歌) — a genre of Baiduist verse that emerged in the Sinchew settlements — combined the devotional imagery of Baiduism with the personal grief of the expelled settlers, and the genre became the foundational literature of the Sinchew Baiduist community.

On the mainland, the Tianist literary establishment produced its own body of post-expulsion literature that celebrated the purification of the kingdom and the restoration of the Tianist order. The court chronicles of Tianxiao's reign presented the expulsion as a necessary act of state preservation, and the Tianist martyrology — the literature of the Underground Sutras that had been produced during Foyuan's reign — was canonised as the official narrative of the Tianist church's suffering and resilience.

The constitutional transition produced a new literature of political theory. The merchants and scholars who had engineered the Merchants' Revolution wrote treatises on the nature of constitutional government, the rights of the assembly, and the proper relationship between the throne and the legislature. The political literature of the period, which drew on both Tianist and Baiduist intellectual traditions, produced a body of constitutional thought that would shape Zong political theory for centuries.

== Legacy ==

The period from 1606 to 1711 transformed Zong from a crisis-ridden autocracy into a constitutional monarchy with a global colonial empire. The expulsion and the Baidu People's Rebellion, intended to eliminate the Baiduist presence from the kingdom, instead produced a Baiduist diaspora that transformed the Sinchew colony into a major population centre with its own cultural and political identity. The Sinchew War, intended to subjugate the Baiduist settlers, instead produced a compromise that granted the settlers a degree of self-governance that would become the foundation for the colony's increasing autonomy.

The Merchants' Revolution and the creation of the General Assembly completed the constitutional evolution that had begun with the concessions forced on Emperor Tianren after the Yuanlong restoration. The constitutional monarchy, while not a full parliamentary system, established the institutional framework that would shape Zong's political development for the following centuries. The General Assembly, with its non-royal representatives and its legislative authority, became the forum for the commercial and political interests that would drive the kingdom's expansion in the 18th century.

The period also marked the beginning of the end of the Zong colonial monopoly in the New World. The Second Lurandian Native Wars demonstrated that the colonial settlements could not expand indefinitely into the interior without provoking armed resistance. The end of the Secret Trade, under pressure from the Marosan alliance, marked the limits of the colonial enterprise's willingness to tolerate illicit practices. The outbreak of the First War of the Isthmus in 1711 signalled the beginning of a new era of colonial conflict between the Illypnian and Fosian powers that would reshape the New World.

